{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791137996,"nonce":"X5JFEzYaek8zjXnHffbJ+qOOplE=","title":"Coverage hung once in blinc_core's tests; it is the only job running them as threads","message":"On 2026-10-04 the Coverage job on c6531f4a ran 1h37m against a normal 8\nto 10 minutes and had to be cancelled. It did NOT reproduce: a re-run of\nthe same commit passed in normal time, and all five jobs are green. So\nthis is a flake, not a defect in that commit. Recorded so the next\noccurrence is not investigated from scratch.\n\n\nThe cancelled job's log shows every test binary passing until\nblinc_core's unit tests started, then silence for over an hour. The\norphan process killed at cancellation was the blinc_core test binary.\n\n\ncargo llvm-cov runs cargo test, which executes a binary's tests as\nparallel THREADS in one process. The CI job uses nextest, which gives\neach test its OWN PROCESS.\n\nblinc_core keeps a process-global\nGLOBAL_GRAPH: LazyLock\u003cArc\u003cMutex\u003cReactiveGraph\u003e\u003e\u003e, used from 12 call\nsites, and drain_deferred_writes loops until a shared queue empties,\ncalling each closure it pops. Under nextest every test gets a fresh\nprivate graph. Under cargo test they all share one, and a deadlock on\nthat mutex or a livelock in that drain loop is reachable only there.\n\nSo the project does not otherwise test the mode Coverage runs in. That\nis a structural gap regardless of whether it caused this particular\nhang.\n\n\n- The commit's own changes. 027b4abc is WGSL and c6531f4a is\n  blinc_layout; neither touches blinc_core or its tests.\n- The environment. The CI job runs the same tests on the same commit\n  under the same xvfb and passed in normal time.\n- Local reproduction. The blinc_core test binary was run 60 times at\n  --test-threads=16 on macOS with no hang, so it is either\n  Linux-specific, specific to instrumentation timing, or a stall.\n\n\nCancel the run first: logs are not served while a job is in progress,\nand cancelling makes them available, which is how the blinc_core\nlocation was found. Then compare against a re-run of the same commit\nbefore suspecting the code. If it recurs on a commit that does touch\nblinc_core, the shared graph and the drain loop are where to look.","files":null}]}